::STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
Which data set is most appropriate for a normal model?
{
=A. Values roughly symmetric around one center with no strong outliers#Correct. Normal models work best for roughly symmetric, mound-shaped data without strong outliers.
~B. Values with two far-apart clusters#Two clusters suggest the data may combine different groups, so a single normal model may not fit well.
~C. Values with a strong right skew#Strong skew means the normal model may misrepresent percentages.
~D. Values with one extreme outlier#An extreme outlier can distort the mean and standard deviation.
}

::STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
A data set has mean 50 and standard deviation 5. What interval is one standard deviation above and below the mean?
{
~A. 40 to 60#This is two standard deviations from the mean, not one.
=B. 45 to 55#Correct. One standard deviation from the mean is 50 - 5 to 50 + 5.
~C. 50 to 55#This only goes above the mean and ignores the lower side.
~D. 0 to 5#This names the standard deviation size but not the interval around the mean.
}

::STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
For a roughly normal data set, about what percent of values are within one standard deviation of the mean?
{
~A. About 50%#Half the data is not the empirical-rule estimate for one standard deviation.
~B. About 95%#About 95% is within two standard deviations, not one.
=C. About 68%#Correct. The empirical rule says about 68% are within one standard deviation.
~D. About 100%#Normal models do not place every value within one standard deviation.
}

::STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
Why must a data set be checked before fitting a normal model?
{
~A. Because every data set has the same standard deviation#Standard deviation changes from data set to data set.
~B. Because normal models only work for categorical data#Normal models are for numerical data, not categorical data.
~C. Because the mean is never used in a normal model#The mean is central to fitting a normal model.
=D. Because not every data set is symmetric or mound-shaped#Correct. Shape and outliers determine whether a normal model is reasonable.
}

::STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L05_GuidedPractice_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
A normal model has mean 80 and standard deviation 10. What value is two standard deviations above the mean?
{
=A. 100#Correct. Two standard deviations above is 80 + 2(10) = 100.
~B. 90#90 is one standard deviation above the mean.
~C. 70#70 is one standard deviation below the mean.
~D. 60#60 is two standard deviations below the mean.
}

